o the Jewish community in Toronto. This includes insufficient police protection, inadequate support for Jewish-owned businesses, and a general lack of responsiveness to growing concerns. Given this situation, it seems only reasonable to request that Jewish residents in Toronto be exempt from the upcoming property tax increase.\n\nPolice Protection:\nAs highlighted by @DahliaKurtz, there are claims that police have been directed to prioritize the protection of pro-Hamas protesters, despite the fact that these demonstrations are causing fear and disruption in the city, particularly in Jewish neighborhoods.\n\nTTC Safety Concerns:\nThe interim CEO of the TTC has publicly requested police assistance to address antisemitic graffiti and ensure the safety of all riders. Despite this, it’s evident that the TTC remains unsafe for Jewish passengers.\n\nDowntown Core Access:\nJewish residents do not feel they are receiving equal protection or access to the downtown area, especially when protests are permitted to disrupt daily life and foster an atmosphere of hostility.\n\nCommunity Engagement:\nDuring your recent AMA, Jewish constituents were reportedly blocked from participating, with legitimate questions going unanswered. Many, including myself, were excluded without clear justification.\n\nGiven the lack of action on these critical issues, we feel marginalized in the city we call home. Therefore, until tangible steps are taken to support the Jewish community, we believe it’s fair to request exemption from the upcoming property tax increase.\n\nAs things stand, many Jewish residents are left feeling unwelcome, either considering leaving Toronto or waiting for new leadership that represents the interests of all communities equally.\n\nSincerely,\nToronto', 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': [{'id_str': '1679527353259532288', 'name': 'Mayor Olivia Chow 🇨🇦', 'screen_name': 'MayorOliviaChow', 'indices': [23, 39]}, {'id_str': '49091922', 'n